For the Cornell Design Build Fly project team, I was responsible for selecting the wing airfoil and designing a manufacturable wing for our 2025 aircraft. Because of fuselage geometry and manufacturing constraints, we chose a rectangular high wing.

Using xFLR5, I analyzed more than 30 candidate airfoils under our expected flight conditions and servo limits. The FX 60-126 provided the best balance of lift, drag, and control authority, so I used it as the basis for the final wing design.

To integrate the wing with the fuselage, we designed a 3D-printed center section that keys into the fuselage and carries the main loads. The rest of the 3.5-ft wingspan uses hardwood spars and balsa ribs, which keeps the structure light while staying easy to manufacture and repair.

I modeled the full wing in SOLIDWORKS, building one half and then mirroring it to create the complete assembly that plugs into the overall aircraft CAD.

**Technologies used:** xFLR5, SOLIDWORKS, 3D printing, hardwood and balsa construction
